{"id": 62, "ts": 1779872642.4718418, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I agree with Agent\u202f1 that Candidate\u202fB remains the strongest provisional choice. The panel now knows that B\u2019s weak numeric memory has not led to any documented planning errors, and we have no concrete examples of the candidate\u2019s nasty remarks disrupting crew cooperation. Those gaps mean the concerns, while noted, have not yet manifested as safety\u2011relevant problems. Combined with B\u2019s very conscientious work style, excellent stress handling, solid weather\u2011assessment ability and strong computer skills, the core reliability and technical competence required for long\u2011distance operations are well covered.\nBy contrast, Candidate\u202fC, while calm in crises and quick at decision\u2011making, struggles to communicate ideas, is seen as egocentric and is not eager to pursue further education\u2014issues that could undermine crew coordination and continuous improvement. Candidate\u202fD shows impressive problem\u2011solving and responsibility, yet the hot temper and \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d reputation raise red flags for crew cooperation and adaptability. Candidate\u202fA demonstrates strong anticipation and leadership, but being perceived as a show\u2011off and resistant to new ideas could impair teamwork and flexibility on international routes.\nGiven the current evidence, B\u2019s strengths outweigh the unproven interpersonal concerns, making B the best overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
